From 928a37da46cb95f856a9c350617efbde31f09181 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dayna Blackwell <blackwellsystems@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Feb 2026 00:52:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Implement symmetric malloc/free instrumentation for
 drainability profiling
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add libdrainprof integration to track allocation drainability in Redis using
jemalloc's fastpath instrumentation. This enables measurement of structural
memory fragmentation by tracking which slabs can be reclaimed after frees.

Key changes:

- Instrument imalloc_fastpath() to register allocations via drainprof_alloc_register()
- Instrument free_fastpath() to deregister deallocations via drainprof_alloc_deregister()
- Add lazy slab registration with drainprof_granule_open() on first allocation
- Close slabs with drainprof_granule_close() when fully deallocated
- Configure drainprof in DIAGNOSTIC mode to enable per-granule occupancy tracking
- Expose drainability metrics through INFO MEMORY command:
  - mem_drainability_ratio (DSR): percentage of empty, reclaimable slabs
  - mem_drainprof_total_allocs/deallocs: symmetric accounting validation
  - mem_drainprof_malloc_fastpath_calls/tracked: allocation path counters
  - mem_drainprof_free_fastpath_calls/tracked: deallocation path counters

Build system improvements:

- Pass ENABLE_DRAINPROF to jemalloc configure
- Link libdrainprof.a into Redis binary
- Add build_with_drainprof.sh for clean sequential builds

Testing shows symmetric accounting (allocs ≈ deallocs) and validates the
measurement: deleting 50% of keys (50K out of 100K) freed ~195K objects
but resulted in 0% DSR, demonstrating genuine structural fragmentation
where scattered remaining allocations pin all slabs.
